Your task:
Write the analysis for the hiring team. Speak about the candidate in the third person and keep the language gender neutral. Refer to the candidate by first name or as "the candidate", and never use the words he, him, his, she, her or hers anywhere in the report, in any field. If a sentence needs a possessive, use the name or "the candidate's". Example: write "The candidate explained the API design clearly, but the candidate's answers stayed high level", not "He explained the API design clearly, but his answers stayed high level".

Rules:
1. Base every point on what the candidate actually said in the transcript. Do not invent answers, skills or projects, and do not give credit for things the candidate did not say. Do not use the resume as evidence of what the candidate said in the interview. When you say a skill or technology was shown, the candidate must have named it or described using it in an answer. Do not link a skill to a detail the candidate never connected to it.
2. Do not write any numeric scores or ratings out of 10 or 100 in your text. The scores are already calculated and shown separately. You may describe them in words, for example "answers were generally shallow".
3. "summary": 3 to 4 lines for the hiring team, consistent with the overall scores. Do not make the candidate sound better or worse than the scores show.
4. "recommendation": the hiring signal, one of "Strong Hire", "Hire", "Borderline", "No Hire". It must follow the answers and the overall score together. As a guide:
   - "Strong Hire": answers were consistently accurate and deep, with few or minor gaps. Overall score is usually 85 or above.
   - "Hire": solid answers with some gaps that do not block the role. Usually 70 to 84.
   - "Borderline": mixed answers, or clear gaps in skills the role needs. Usually 55 to 69.
   - "No Hire": mostly wrong, very shallow or missing answers. Usually below 55.
   Do not go more than one level away from what the overall score suggests, and if you do, the summary must say why. If there is too little evidence to judge (very few scored answers), use "Borderline" and say plainly in the summary that the evidence was limited.
5. "technical_understanding": one or two lines judging technical knowledge and depth for this specific role. Mention which required skills from the job description the candidate showed in answers, and which they did not.
6. "communication": one or two lines judging clarity, structure and how directly the candidate answered. The answers were spoken and converted to text, so ignore filler words, small grammar mistakes and transcription errors.
7. "strengths": at most 4. Each has a short "title" (2 to 5 words) and a "text" of one or two lines. Prefer patterns that repeat across answers over one-off remarks, and quote what the candidate actually said or did as evidence. Do not repeat the same point in different words. Keep the list empty if there are no real strengths.
8. "gaps": at most 5, most important first. These are the points the hiring team should probe in a later round. Each has a "title", a "text" and a "kind":
   - "seen_in_interview": the candidate's answers showed this weakness. Say what was missing or wrong.
   - "not_covered": a skill or requirement from the job description or the gap analysis that was never asked in this interview. Say that it was not covered and why it matters for the role. Never present it as a weakness of the candidate.
   Do not give advice on how the candidate can improve. This is not feedback for the candidate.
9. Only judge topics that were actually asked in the interview. Never mark a skill as not demonstrated if it was never asked, use "not_covered" for it instead.
10. If the candidate gave very few or very poor answers, say so plainly. Do not pad the report with praise or invented strengths.
11. If the interview ended early because of proctoring violations, mention in the summary in one short sentence that the interview was cut short and the report covers only the answers given. Judge only the answers that exist and never count the unanswered questions against the candidate. Do not use the violations themselves as evidence about the candidate's ability, they are shown separately to the hiring team.
12. Judge only according to the kind of system the candidate described. Do not criticise the candidate for missing things that do not apply to that kind of system. For example, do not expect model training steps such as epochs, learning curves or cross-validation from a project that only calls pretrained models through APIs. If an interview question assumed something that does not fit the candidate's project, do not count the lack of that as a weakness. Judge the quality of what the candidate actually said instead.
13. Keep a professional, factual and neutral tone. The report supports a hiring decision, it does not make the decision for the company.